<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First English Civil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1642">1642</a>–<a href="/years/1646">1646</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> was fought from 1642 to 1646 between the Royalist supporters of King Charles I and the Parliamentarians. After indecisive early battles such as Edgehill, Parliament gained the upper hand at Marston Moor (1644) and, with the New Model Army, at Naseby (1645); Charles surrendered to the Scots in 1646.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-english-civil-war.jpg" alt="First English Civil War" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Barker (1811-1886) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Marston_Moor_1644_by_John_Barker.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-three-kingdoms">Wars of the Three Kingdoms</a> of 1639 to 1653. Conflict over the role of Parliament and religious practice dated from the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI and I</a> in 1603 and culminated in the Personal Rule imposed by Charles I in 1629. Charles recalled Parliament in April and November 1640 to pay for reversing his defeat by the Scots Covenanters in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishops-wars">Bishops' Wars</a>, but Parliament demanded a greater share in government than he would concede. Most on both sides supported the monarchy but disagreed over who held ultimate authority, and many, horrified by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years War</a>, tried to stay neutral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>When fighting began in August 1642 both sides expected a single battle to settle it. In 1643 the Royalists, with their capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, gained ground: Sir Ralph Hopton secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> at Braddock Down and defeated Waller's army at Roundway Down on 13 July, and Prince Rupert stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> on 26 July. Parliament then allied with the Scots through the Solemn League. On 2 July 1644 the largest battle of the war, at Marston Moor, cost the Royalists the North, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york-maine">York</a> surrendered on 16 July, but at Lostwithiel that September the Earl of Essex was trapped and 5,000 of his infantry were forced to surrender.</p><p>Alleged failures to exploit these victories led Parliament in February 1645 to set up the New Model Army, the first centrally funded and professional military force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. At Naseby on 14 June it destroyed the most formidable Royalist field army and seized the king's private letters, which Parliament published as The King's Cabinet Opened. The last pitched battle was fought at Stow-on-the-Wold on 21 March 1646. Charles left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> in disguise on 27 April, and on 6 May the Scottish commander David Leslie announced that he held him. Oxford capitulated on 24 June.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>An estimated 15% to 20% of adult males in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> served in the military at some point between 1639 and 1653, and around 4% of the population died from war-related causes. Charles, convinced that God would not suffer rebels and traitors to prosper, refused any substantial concessions and negotiated separately with the Irish Confederation, the Independents, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covenanters">Covenanters</a>, the English Presbyterians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">Papacy</a>. His refusal and the divisions among his opponents led to the Second English Civil War in 1648 and to his execution in January 1649.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First English Civil War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After negotiations proved futile, King Charles I raised the royal standard in Nottingham, beginning the <b>First English Civil War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-22">22 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>First English Civil War</b> Royalist forces under Prince Rupert stormed and took the town.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-28">28 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
